  "notes": "The played low-end and guitar layer that separates g-funk from sample rap, modelled as a session model: it has no drum programming and no harmony of its own, because the research says they realize other people's harmony over minor and mixolydian vamps. So `chords` is inherited whole, deliberately, and everything this entry owns is bass, guitar and feel. The bass is the point — live-recorded, melodic, syncopated, slides and dead notes, and mixed loud — which is `followRootsProb` at 0.3, `glideProb` at 0.7 and the widest `passingTones` set on this axis, blue third and blue seventh included. Their phrasing is documented as sitting a few milliseconds behind the programmed kick, so `quantizeStrength` is 0.68 and `velocityVar` is high. ⚠ The research gives the register as E1–G2, which is exactly 15 semitones; [28,45] is that window with two extra semitones at the top so the bass generator has room to be distinct, and the floor is unchanged. ⚠ Their parts continue through sections where the synths drop — that is why they are the continuity layer, so `countermelody.densityRatio` is 0.65 and `transitions.dropOutBeats.prob` is cut to 0.25. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it high-medium: the players and the method are documented, the per-record tempo band is catalog-level.",
